// NEGATIVE TEST -- N2-10 shape two: `#[name(args)]`.
//
// THE REFUSAL IS THE EVIDENCE THAT IT LEXED. Reaching
// `unknown attribute `frobnicate`` means the parser consumed `(a, b(c))` and
// the closing `]` as part of ONE attribute and then looked the name up. A
// scanner that stopped at the first `)` would have left `]` behind and failed
// with a different message entirely, which is what makes this a test of the
// argument list and not a duplicate of unknown_attribute.pd.
//
// The nesting is deliberate: `b(c)` inside `(...)` is the case that
// distinguishes balanced counting from "scan to the first `)`".
#[frobnicate(a, b(c))]
fn f() -> i64 {
    return 1;
}

fn main() {
    print_int(f());
}
